\section developer_template_uses_cases_sec Overview

Use cases for C++ class templates in PSCF generally fall into one or 
more of the following categories:
<ol type="A">
    <li>
    <b> Container templates </b> : Arrays and other containers are
    defined as specializations of class templates in which the type
    of each element is a template parameter. 
    </li>

    <li>
    <b> Templatizing the dimension of space </b> :
    In code for periodic systems, the dimension of space is always 
    treated as a template parameter, denoted by D. 
    </li>

    <li>
    <b> Algorithm templates </b> : Algorithms that are used in two or
    more PSCF programs are sometimes implemented in skeleton form by 
    a class template.
    
    </li>
    <li>
    <b> Base classes for analogous classes used in different programs </b>:
    The Rpc and Rpg namespaces contain many pairs of closely analogous
    classes that play analogous roles in the implementation of the 
    pscf_rpc CPU program and pscf_rpg GPU-accelerated program for
    periodic systems. To reduce code duplication, both classes in such 
    pairs are often derived from instantiations of a shared base class 
    template that is defined in directory src/rp. 
    </li>
</ol>

These cases are not mutually exclusive. Specifically, cases C and D
often both apply to the same template: A skeleton version of an 
algorithm may be implemented in a class template that is used to 
define base classes for more specialized subclasses.

Each of these use cases is discussed in more detail below.

\subsection developer_template_uses_container_sub A. Container templates

The most conventional use of templates in PSCF is their use to define
generic data containers. This is analogous to the use of templates in the
C++ standard library to define container templatess such as std::vector.
Rather than using the container templates defined in the standard library,
however, PSCF relies primarily on a set of container templates that are
distributed with the package.

For example, most one-dimensional arrays used in PSCF are defined as
instantiations of a class template named Util::DArray. This template takes 
the data type of a single element as a template parameter, so that
Util::DArray<double> is an array of double precision real numbers. The 
source code for DArray and other container templates used in PSCF is 
located in directory src/util/containers.

The DArray class template defines a simple dynamically allocated array
that allows individual elements to be access via an overloaded subscript
[] operator, using the same syntax for this as that used by built-in
C-arrays.  A DArray holds data in a contiguous block of memory that may
be dynamically allocated by invoking a member function named "allocate" 
at some point after construction.  The decision to rely primarily on 
DArray and other home-grown containers throughout PSCF was based in part 
on a preference for this type of interface for memory management, in which 
allocation is separate from both object construction and addition of 
elements. Note that this is different from the interfaces used by either 
a std::vector (which is a dynamic array whose size can grow at any time 
by pushing elements to the end of the array) or by std::array (for which 
the array size must be known at compile time). The container classes used 
by PSCF also provide a mechanism to either enable run-time checking of 
array bounds, to facilitate debugging of errors, or disable these 
additional checks to maximize performance in production code.

\subsection developer_template_uses_D_sub B. Dimension of space

Throughout the code for systems with periodic boundary conditions, the
the dimension of space, denoted by D, is treated as a template parameter.  
Almost all of the classes used defined namespaces Prdc, Rpc and Rpg are 
defined as instantiations of class templates in which D is a template 
parameter, and often the only template parameter.  For example, classes 
used by pscf_rpc to represent a block within a block polymer and an entire 
polymer are defined as instantiations of templates that are declared within 
namespace Pscf::Rpc
as "template <int D> class Block" and "template <int D> class Polymer",
respectively. For each class template for which D is the only template
parameter, the PSCF build system actually explicitly instantiates (i.e.,
compiles code for) three specializations corresponding to the three
possible values of D = 1, 2, and 3.

The ability to use different values for the spatial dimension D is
primarily relevant to SCFT calculations of periodic structures. In this
context,  D is used to specify the number of directions or coordinates
along which some structure of interest is periodic. For example, the SCFT
solution of a lamellar phase is described by fields that are periodic with
respect to one coordinate (the coordinate perpendicular to the layers),
and independent of the remaining two orthogonal coordinates. As a result,
SCFT calculation for a lamellar phase are normally performed by PSCF
using specializations with D=1 for all class templates in which D appears
as a template parameter.  PS-FTS calculations are instead usually performed
using D=3 even for simulations of a lamellar or columnar phases, because
the fluctuations that are sampled in such stochastic simulations can depend 
on all three physical coordinates, even if the average field configuration 
exhibits a periodic dependence on only one or two coordinates.

The decision to treat D as a template parameter in PSCF was made to 
simplify design and improve performance of parts of the code that deal 
with unit cells, crystallography, and periodic fields. Treating D as a 
template parameter allows algorithms that involve description of unit 
cells, reciprocal lattice vectors, symmetry operations, space groups, 
and mesh dimensions to be implemented using data structures that 
represent D-dimensional vectors and D \f$ \times \f$ D matrices using 
memory blocks whose sizes are known at compile time, and that can thus 
be created on the stack, rather than allocating memory for these small 
data structures on the heap at run time. 

\subsection developer_template_uses_algorithm_sub C. Algorithm Templates

Some algorithms and data structures that are used in several different 
programs or contexts are implemented as class templates. The use of a 
template allows the use of different class types with analogous 
interfaces to be used in different instantiations of such a template.

The Pscf::AmIteratorTmpl class template is an important example of 
this sort of template. This template implements a skeleton version of the 
Anderson mixing algorithm for iteratively solving a system of nonlinear 
equations. Classes that are derived from instantiations of this template 
are used to implement Anderson mixing SCFT iteration algorithms in all 
three PSCF programs, and are also used in the the pscf_rpc and pscf_rpc 
programs to implement "compressor" algorithms that are used in PS-FTS 
simulations find field configurations that satisfy a partial saddle-point
condition. Every instantiation of the AmIteratorTmpl class template is a 
subclass of a base class that is specified by a template parameter. The 
argument assigned to this parameter is normally taken to be an SCFT 
"Iterator" or PS-FTS "Compressor" base class that is defined in a 
particular program-level namespace.  The AmIteratorTmpl template also 
has a second template parameter which is the type of the array container 
used to represent residual vector (the vector of errors) and state 
vectors (the vector of unknown variables) that are used in the algorithm. 
Instantiations of the template that are designed to use a GPU may use an 
argument for this template parameter that is a container that allocates
memory in global GPU memory, while instantiations that are used in CPU
programs use a container that uses CPU memory. The AmIteratorTmpl 
template delegates the computation of the residual vector, retrieval 
of the state vector, and modification of the state vector to pure 
virtual functions that must be implemented by subclasses.

The directory src/pscf/solvers also contains a set of class templates
named "PropagatorTmpl", "BlockTmpl", "PolymerTmpl" and "MixtureTmpl" 
that are designed to be used as base classes for classes that are used 
to solve the modified diffusion equation in each of the PSCF programs. 

\subsection developer_template_uses_base_sub D. Base classes for analogous
classes used by different programs

The C++ version of PSCF was designed as package that could contain code
for several different executable programs.  Currently, PSCF contains code 
for three programs (pscf_1d, pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg, respectively) that are 
constructed from source code defined in three corresonding program-level 
namespaces (Pscf:R1d, Pscf::Rpc, and Pscf::Rpg, respectively). These three 
programs differ because they are either designed for different types of 
spatial domain and boundary conditions (1D vs. periodic) or use different 
hardware (CPU vs. GPU). 

By convention, named entitites (classes, class templates, or functions) 
that are defined in any one any one program-level namespace are used in 
only one executable file, and may not use entities defined another program 
level namespace. Classes and other entitites that can be used in two or 
more executable must instead be defined in the Util, Pscf or Pscf::Prdc 
namespace, which may be accessed in any of the program-level namespaces.

Many of the basic data structures and algorithms used in the three
different PSCF programs are closely analogous, but are implemented using
different classes for analogous purposes. Closely analogous classes
used in different executables are generally given the same class name
but are defined in different program-level namespaces. For example, each
of the three program-level namespaces uses a class or class template
named Block that represents one block within a block polymer. The
executable pscf_1d uses a class named Block that is defined in namespace
Pscf::R1d, while pscf_rpc uses a class template named Block that is
defined namespaces Pscf::Rpc, which takes D as a template parameter.
The convention that prohibits code in one program-level namespace from
using names defined in another allows names to be re-used in this way
without causing name clashes.

Many of the differences between analogous classes used by different
executables arise from the fact that different executables use different
classes to represent functions of position, also known as fields.
Throughout the code in namespace Pscf::R1d that is used to construct
pscf_1d, fields are represented using a simple 1D DArray<double>
container in which each element represents the value of a real field
on a node of a regular 1D grid. Within code in namespace Rpc that is
used to construct pscf_rpc, real-valued fields for systems that are
periodic in D dimensions are represented by a template specialization
RField\<D\> of a class template RField that is defined in namespace
Pscf::Prdc::Cpu, for which D is the only template parameter. Within code
in namespace Rpg that is used to construct pscf_rpg, each such real-valued
field in D-dimensional space is instead represented by a specialization
RField\<D\> of another class template named RField that is defined in
namespace Pscf::Prdc::Cuda.  The class templates Pscf::Prdc::Cuda::RField
and Pscf::Prdc::Cpu::RField are closely analogous **except** for the fact
that the block of contiguous memory that actually contains the field data
stored by a class Pscf::Prdc::Cuda::RField\<D\> is allocated in global GPU
memory, while the memory used by Pscf::Cpu::RField\<D\> is allocated in CPU
memory. As one consequence of this difference, individual elements of an
Pscf::Prdc::Cuda::RField\<D\> container are not directly accessible from
the CPU via the subscript operator. This difference necessarily causes
other differences in code that use these two classes. Because many other
classes used by each program directly or indirectly own multiple instances
of the class that represents a field, the use of different data types to
represent fields ended up infecting the definitions of many other classes,
forcing a design in which different programs are based on distinct but
analogous sets of class definitions.

The source code for analogous classes used by different executable
programs is often itself closely analogous, except for their use of
distinct data types for analogous purposes.  These analogies are
particularly strong between the code defined in Pscf::Rpc and
Pscf::Ppg, because the corresponding pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg programs are
designed to solve the same problem using different hardware, and to
provide identical features to the user. To avoid unnecessary code
duplication, analogous classes defined in these two program-level
namespaces are sometimes derived from different specializations of
a single class template that is defined in Pscf or Pscf::Prdc, in which
the base class template contains template code for data structures and
algorithms that appear in both implementations.

Class templates that are designed to be used as base classes appear in the
following locations:

   - The directory src/pscf/solvers contains a set of class templates
     named PropagatorTmpl, BlockTmpl, PolymerTmpl, and MixtureTmpl that
     are used as base classes for classes that are used to solve the
     ideal gas problems in every PSCF program.

   - The directories src/prdc/field, src/prdc/solvers, and src/prdc/system
     contain templates for a variety of class templates that are each
     designed to be used as base classes for a pair of closely analogous
     classes used in pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg, respectively, to treat problems
     with periodic boundary conditions.

<BR>
